export const lpContent={
 ball:{
  title:'The Ball Lesson',one:'Teach the first play word with a roll-and-chase game that ends with the child rolling BACK.',
  seoTitle:'Ball Lesson for Toddlers — Teaching the First Play Word (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 1–3: how to teach the word ball with a slow-roll game, a chase and a turn-taking finish that toddlers beg to repeat.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "ball" the way toddlers learn best: the word, the object, the game — in that order, with the rolling done by both of you.',
  what:'A ball is a round toy that rolls. It is usually the first play object a toddler can name, because it moves, it responds and it never gets old.',
  mean:'The ball lesson is really two lessons: the word "ball" attaches to a loved object, and the rolling game plants turn-taking — I roll, you roll — long before sharing is taught.',
  know:'You need the ball, a stretch of floor and three lines: the word before the action ("ball!"), the action after the word (roll), and the pause after your turn — waiting is where the toddler\u2019s turn is born.',
  why:'Ball is a high-frequency, low-frustration word: the object is exciting, the game is instant and a failed roll just rolls again. Words with happy loops get learned and kept.',
  dev:'First-word vocabulary, visual tracking (the eyes follow the roll), reaching and grasping, and the earliest back-and-forth rhythm that conversation will borrow.',
  vocab:'ball, roll, chase, catch, my turn, your turn',
  teach:'1) Hold the ball still at the toddler\u2019s eye level and say "ball" — twice, slowly. Let a hand land on it.\n2) Roll it one slow stride away. Say "roll!" as it goes — word and motion glued together.\n3) Let the chase happen. The toddler fetches; you celebrate with the word: "you found the ball!"\n4) Open your hands and ask: "roll to me?" Wait. The wait is the lesson — a returned roll is a conversation.\n5) Finish on a win, not a fade: two more rolls, then "ball sleeps now" as you park it on the shelf.',
  say:'"Ball! This is the ball. Watch — the ball rolls! Roll, roll, roll… chase it! And now: roll the ball to me. And I roll it back — my turn, your turn. Ball is the best game."',
  notice:'On this card the word "ball" sits under a beach ball in four bright colors — red, yellow, green, blue — a picture as round and simple as the toy itself.',
  ask:'"Where is the ball?" "Can you roll the ball?" "Whose turn is it — my turn or your turn?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say "baaa!", roll it back on request, or push it, sit on it and laugh — all three are the lesson working.',
  real:'Balls turn up everywhere: the market pile, a neighbour\u2019s yard, the phone screen when a match is on. Name each one — "ball!" — and the word picks up fans all over town.',
  play:'Roll-and-Freeze: roll three times with turns, then call "freeze!" — both hands stop the ball. Freeze makes the stopping a game, and a child who can stop a game can start one tomorrow.',
  check:'Ask: "What is this?" (ball) "Show me roll." (a push) "Now MY turn or YOUR turn?" A point, a push and a giggle — three ticks, lesson done.'},
 blanket:{
  title:'The Blanket Lesson',one:'Teach the word that makes bedtime predictable — with a teddy tucked in first.',
  seoTitle:'Blanket Lesson for Toddlers — The Sleepy-Time Word (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 1–3: how to teach "blanket" with a teddy peekaboo game that rehearses bedtime while everyone is still smiling.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "blanket" away from bedtime first — the word gets its own happy game, so when it appears at the crib rail it is already a friend.',
  what:'A blanket is the soft cover that goes over the body for sleeping — folded by day, spread over the crib by night, and known to every toddler as the cozy part of bedtime.',
  mean:'Blanket is a routine word: it does not describe a thing so much as predict an event. Routine words are the toddler\u2019s schedule made speakable.',
  know:'You need the blanket, a willing teddy and one rule — the game happens when nobody is tired. A word practised in daylight works at night; a word introduced at bedtime works a week later.',
  why:'Bedtime friction is usually surprise friction. The word "blanket" said at the same moment nightly turns the cover into a countdown the toddler\u2019s body believes.',
  dev:'Routine vocabulary, object permanence (things go under and come back), gentle-hand practice, and the self-soothing habit of a cover that means rest.',
  vocab:'blanket, soft, cozy, tucked in, night-night, sleep',
  teach:'1) Bring the blanket to the play floor: "blanket! Soft and cozy."\n2) Tuck the teddy in: "night-night, teddy" — cover him fully, wait two seconds.\n3) Lift the blanket: "good MORNING, teddy!" The reveal is the joke; do it five times.\n4) Offer the turn: the toddler covers the teddy. Sloppy tucks are perfect tucks.\n5) At bedtime tonight, hold the same blanket and use the same words — "blanket, cozy, night-night" — so the game and the crib speak one language.',
  say:'"Blanket! The soft, cozy blanket. Look — teddy is cold! Tuck him in… night-night, teddy. Shhh, he is sleeping. And… good morning, teddy! Again! You tuck him in this time."',
  notice:'On this card the word "blanket" sits under a folded blue blanket — folded the way it waits on the crib all day before it works at night.',
  ask:'"Where does the blanket go?" (on the bed / over teddy) "Who is sleeping under the blanket?" "Is the blanket soft or hard?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say "nigh-nigh" while tucking, pat the blanket gently, or fetch it at bedtime unprompted — the routine word landing where it lives.',
  real:'Blankets live on sofas, in prams, in grandma\u2019s house. Each sighting is a free repetition: "blanket! Cozy one." The word travels; the routine follows.',
  play:'Teddy Tuck-In: three tucks, three wake-ups, then swap roles — the toddler tucks, YOU say night-night. Reversing the roles doubles the giggles and the practice.',
  check:'Ask: "Where does the blanket go?" (on the bed) "Show me soft." (a gentle pat) "Who sleeps under it?" A pat, a point, a name — the sleepy word is won.'},
 bookshelf:{
  title:'The Bookshelf Lesson',one:'Teach where books live — and make putting away the last page of every story.',
  seoTitle:'Bookshelf Lesson for Toddlers — Where the Books Live (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 2–3: how to teach "bookshelf" with a choose-and-return ritual that makes the shelf the start and finish of story time.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "bookshelf" as a place with a job: books sleep here, stories start here, and the walk home to the shelf is part of reading.',
  what:'A bookshelf is the shelf where books live between readings — in this room, a low shelf the toddler can reach, so choosing and returning are both the child\u2019s own work.',
  mean:'The bookshelf teaches belonging: books have a home, stories have a start line, and the child who puts a book back owns the whole reading loop.',
  know:'You need a reachable shelf, two or three books and the two-line ritual: "pick a book" (start) and "book goes home" (finish). The parent\u2019s job is to point, name and wait.',
  why:'A named shelf turns tidy-up from instruction into identity — "we are the family whose books go home." That identity survives every room the child will ever read in.',
  dev:'Choice-making (a real executive-function seed), carrying carefully, matching object to place, and the finish-a-task habit that tidying quietly builds.',
  vocab:'bookshelf, book, pick, put back, home, story',
  teach:'1) Stand at the shelf together: "bookshelf! The books live here."\n2) Invite the choice: "pick a book — any book." Wait through the deliberation; choosing is thinking.\n3) Read anywhere — floor, lap, cushion. The shelf only bookends the story.\n4) The return march: "story finished — book goes home on the bookshelf!" Walk it back together, spine first.\n5) Close the loop with ceremony: "night-night, book. See you tomorrow." Books tucked in get chosen again.',
  say:'"Bookshelf! This is where the books live. Pick a book… that one! Good choice. Let\u2019s read… and finished! Now the book goes home — on the bookshelf. Night-night, book."',
  notice:'On this card the word "bookshelf" sits under a blue shelf with books lying on three shelves — a library the exact size of a toddler\u2019s reach.',
  ask:'"Where do the books live?" "Can you pick a book?" "Where does the book go when the story is finished?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say "book!" while reaching, march the book back to the shelf, or pat it goodnight — each one is the ritual taking root.',
  real:'Shelves are everywhere once named: the shop shelf, the library cart, grandma\u2019s bookcase. "Books live on shelves" generalizes the tidy habit to every room in its life.',
  play:'Shelf Surprise: hide one small toy among the books and ask the shelf-keeper to find it — "check the bookshelf!" The shelf becomes a place worth visiting, and visitors keep it tidy.',
  check:'Ask: "Where do books live?" "Pick a book." (a reach) "Finished — where does it go?" Home. Point. Done.'},
 bottle:{
  title:'The Bottle Lesson',one:'Teach the first mealtime word — then let the teddy be fed by the expert.',
  seoTitle:'Bottle Lesson for Toddlers — The First Mealtime Word (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 1–3: how to teach "bottle" with a hold-and-drink routine and a doll-feeding game that rehearses mealtimes.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "bottle" at real feeds first, then hands the tool over to a doll — because a toddler who feeds a teddy is rehearsing being fed, and rehearsed routines go smoother.',
  what:'A bottle is the small milk container with the yellow lid that many toddlers drink from — often the very first food word a family uses a dozen times a day.',
  mean:'Bottle is a comfort word wrapped around a routine: naming it at every feed builds the mealtime vocabulary the cup, the plate and the spoon will inherit.',
  know:'You need the actual bottle (or the doll\u2019s empty one) and the same two-beat script each time: name it before, name it after. "Bottle time… all gone!"',
  why:'A named routine is a calmer routine: the word lands before the milk does, and a toddler who can predict the feed arrives at it peacefully.',
  dev:'Mealtime vocabulary, holding and tipping (hand strength and control), cause and effect — tip, swallow, empty — and pretend-play seeding for the teddy stage.',
  vocab:'bottle, milk, drink, hold, yummy, all gone',
  teach:'1) Before the feed, hold the bottle up: "bottle! Time to drink."\n2) Guide two small hands around it — the hold is half the lesson.\n3) During: "drink, drink… yummy milk." The same words, every feed.\n4) After: "all gone!" — and let the toddler hand you the empty bottle. Handing in is a real job.\n5) Later at play, offer the doll bottle: "teddy is hungry — feed teddy!" Watch the whole routine replay in small hands.',
  say:'"Bottle! Here is your bottle of milk. Two hands… hold it tight. Drink, drink — yummy! …All gone! Well done. Now, can teddy have a drink too? Feed the teddy!"',
  notice:'On this card the word "bottle" sits under a baby bottle with a yellow lid and the milk line showing — filled exactly to where a small drink stops.',
  ask:'"What is this?" "Where is the bottle?" "Can you feed teddy his bottle?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say "baba," tap the bottle when thirsty, or tip the doll bottle to the teddy\u2019s mouth with a sound effect — feeding vocabulary in action.',
  real:'Bottles appear at feeds, in bags, at grandma\u2019s. Name them all — the word collects them. When the bottle finally retires, the card stays as the record of a first word well loved.',
  play:'Teddy Café: three "customers" (teddy, bunny, dolly) wait for their bottles. The barista feeds each one, on request, with the grown-up naming the order: "bottle for teddy!" Service with babble.',
  check:'Ask: "What is this?" (bottle) "Where is the bottle?" (a point) "Feed teddy!" (a tip) — name, point, tip: the mealtime word is served.'},
 'building-blocks':{
  title:'The Building Blocks Lesson',one:'Teach stacking with turn-taking — and make the crash the best part of the science.',
  seoTitle:'Building Blocks Lesson for Toddlers — Stack, Count, Crash (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 2–3: how to teach "building blocks" with a take-turns tower, counting as you stack and a demolition everyone earned.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "building blocks" through a shared tower — your block, their block, counted aloud — and ends in a crash that is permission, not mischief.',
  what:'Building blocks are the chunky colored blocks that stack into towers — the toddler engineering kit, complete with its own demolition phase.',
  mean:'The block tower is early physics (balance), early maths (counting) and early manners (taking turns) built in one pile. The crash is the laboratory\u2019s favourite experiment: what goes up comes down.',
  know:'You need floor space, a shared tower and the turn rhythm: "my block… your block." Count each landing. Announce the crash BEFORE it happens so the demolition is a plan, not a crime.',
  why:'Blocks teach fail-safe persistence: a tower that falls is not a failure, it is the next game. That attitude is the whole foundation of learning anything harder later.',
  dev:'Grasp and release, hand-eye aim, balance intuition, counting-with-one-to-one-touch, turn-taking and cause-and-effect joy.',
  vocab:'building blocks, stack, tower, up, crash, again',
  teach:'1) Spread the blocks: "building blocks!" — name while the hands find them.\n2) Place the first block: "my block. One."\n3) Offer the second: "your block… two!" Guide wobbles gently; wobble is data.\n4) Count every landing up the tower — the numbers climb with the blocks.\n5) The announcement: "tall tower… ready… CRASH!" Knock it down together, cheer, and start again — the rebuild is where the counting happens twice as happily.',
  say:'"Building blocks! Let\u2019s build a tower. My block — one. Your block — two! My block — three… up, up, up… TALL tower! And now… crash it down! Again, again!"',
  notice:'On this card the words "building blocks" sit under a tower of red, yellow, green and blue blocks — stacked the way every first tower stands: biggest bottom, proudest top.',
  ask:'"How many blocks in our tower?" "Whose turn now?" "What happens if we push it?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say "more!", plant a block crooked and proud, or deliver the crash themselves with a drumroll — all of it is engineering.',
  real:'Towers live beyond the playroom: stone stacks in the garden, cups in the kitchen, sugar packets at the café. "Stack — like building blocks!" keeps the word earning.',
  play:'Knock-Down Countdown: build six high together, then count down 3-2-1 — the toddler delivers the crash on ZERO. Countdowns and crashes: the two best motivators ever invented for turn-taking.',
  check:'Ask: "What are these?" (building blocks) "Stack them up!" (two or more placed) "What comes after tall?" (CRASH) — build, count, demolish: lesson complete.'},
 'changing-table':{
  title:'The Changing Table Lesson',one:'Teach the change-station word so the routine has a name and a script.',
  seoTitle:'Changing Table Lesson for Toddlers — The Diaper Routine (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 2–3: how to teach "changing table" with a same-words-every-time script and a one-job handover that ends the change-time wrestle.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "changing table" as the address of a routine — and gives the toddler one real job per change, which is how changes stop being wrestles.',
  what:'The changing table is the raised station where diaper changes happen — a place with a name, which is exactly what makes the routine predictable.',
  mean:'Toddlers resist surprises more than diaper changes. A named place plus a named sequence (up, lie down, fresh diaper, all clean) converts a wrestle into a script everyone knows.',
  know:'You need the same short script every change and ONE job to hand over: carrying the diaper, peeling a tab, holding the cream. The job is not decoration — it is the cooperation.',
  why:'A routine with an address and a script survives travel, grandparents and growth spurts. The words stay the same even when the world does not.',
  dev:'Routine vocabulary, body awareness (lie down, legs up), handing and fetching, and the first link in the chain that ends at the potty.',
  vocab:'changing table, lie down, diaper, fresh, all clean, done',
  teach:'1) Name the destination as you lift: "changing table! Up we go."\n2) Lay down with the same words: "lie down… there you are."\n3) Hand over the job: "hold the diaper — carry it for me!" The carrier is a helper, not a captive.\n4) Narrate the middle briefly: "fresh diaper… tabs… click."\n5) Finish with the fanfare: "ALL CLEAN!" and lift down — the same words, every single time, until the toddler pre-says them.',
  say:'"Changing table! Up we go. Lie down, little one — there you are. Hold the diaper for me… thank you, helper! Fresh diaper… tabs… ALL CLEAN! Down you hop."',
  notice:'On this card the words "changing table" sit under a blue changing table with yellow shelves — the station itself, ready and waiting.',
  ask:'"Where do diaper changes happen?" "Can you carry the diaper?" "What do we say at the end?" (all clean!)',
  childSay:'Your child might say "table!" on the way, carry the diaper with ceremony, or announce "all clean!" before you do — the script is theirs now.',
  real:'Change stations live in cafés, malls and airports — named, they are familiar ports, not ambushes. The script travels with you.',
  play:'Dolly\u2019s Change: the teddy or dolly gets the full routine at the "little table" — the toddler runs every step and every word. The expert teaching the expert.',
  check:'Ask: "Where do we change your diaper?" "Show me lie down." "What do we say at the end?" Address, pose, fanfare — the routine is owned.'},
 comb:{
  title:'The Comb Lesson',one:'Teach the hair word with a my-turn-your-turn swap that ends the daily battle.',
  seoTitle:'Comb Lesson for Toddlers — Gentle Hair, Shared Turns (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 1–3: how to teach "comb" with a take-turns routine — grown-up strokes, then toddler strokes on a teddy or on you.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "comb" through a shared turn: the grown-up combs three strokes, then hands the comb over — because control shared is a battle halved.',
  what:'A comb is the toothed tool that tidies hair — it appears twice daily, which makes it one of the highest-frequency care words in the room.',
  mean:'Hair care is body-ownership territory. A toddler who is handed the comb learns that their head is theirs AND that routines can be shared — the exact formula for every care routine to come.',
  know:'You need the comb, a willing head and the swap rule: three strokes each, counted out. The toddler\u2019s strokes can land on teddy, on you, or on their own hair — any head counts.',
  why:'The daily wrestle over hair is rarely about hair. It is about control. Sharing the tool converts the comb from something done TO the child into something done BY the family.',
  dev:'Tool grip, gentle-hand control, body-part vocabulary (hair, head), turn-taking and the ticklish business of self-grooming.',
  vocab:'comb, hair, gentle, my turn, your turn, tidy',
  teach:'1) Present the comb: "comb! Time for tidy hair."\n2) Comb three slow strokes on the toddler: "my turn — one… two… three."\n3) Offer the swap: "your turn!" Hand it over handle-first.\n4) Accept any target: their hair, teddy\u2019s fur, your head. Stroke count matters more than stroke target.\n5) Close with the mirror: "all tidy! Look at that hair." The finish line turns a chore into a reveal.',
  say:'"Comb! Time to comb your hair. My turn — one… two… three. Gentle, gentle. Now YOUR turn — comb teddy! Comb my hair! One… two… three. All tidy — look at that!"',
  notice:'On this card the word "comb" sits under a blue comb, teeth pointing the way it travels through hair.',
  ask:'"What is this?" "Whose turn is it?" "Comb teddy\u2019s hair — gently!"',
  childSay:'Your child might say "my turn!", comb your hair with intense concentration, or pat the result in the mirror — control and pride arriving together.',
  real:'Combs appear at the barber, in grandma\u2019s bag, at the pool. Every sighting is a rep: "comb! The hair-tidier." The word and the routine keep each other sharp.',
  play:'Salon Time: teddy, bunny and dolly queue up for the toddler\u2019s salon. Three strokes each, gentle hands, "next customer please!" — the daily comb becomes a business.',
  check:'Ask: "What is this?" (comb) "Show me gentle." (a soft stroke) "Whose turn after mine?" — name, touch, swap: tidy is taught.'},
 crayons:{
  title:'The Crayons Lesson',one:'Teach the drawing words — and ask about the story, never the picture.',
  seoTitle:'Crayons Lesson for Toddlers — First Marks and Big Stories (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 2–3: how to teach "crayons" with a side-by-side scribble session and the one question that grows a storyteller.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "crayons" through a shared scribble — one crayon each, marks on paper, and the golden question: "tell me about your drawing."',
  what:'Crayons are the wax sticks that make colored marks on paper — the first drawing tools, and at this age the marks are scribbles with narratives attached.',
  mean:'Scribbling is pre-writing and the narration is pre-literacy: a child who explains a scribble is learning that marks carry meaning, which is the whole secret of reading waiting downstream.',
  know:'You need paper taped down (the tape IS the trick — no sliding, no floor art), one crayon per hand and the question rule: "tell me about it," never "what is it?"',
  why:'Being asked to narrate makes the child the author. Being asked to identify makes them the test-taker. Same scribble, opposite lessons — choose author.',
  dev:'Tripod grip seeds, mark control (pressure, direction), color words, narrative language and the sitting-focus that pencil work will later borrow.',
  vocab:'crayons, colors, scribble, draw, paper, tell me',
  teach:'1) Tape the paper, open the box: "crayons! Look at the colors — red, yellow, green, blue."\n2) Each choose one: "I pick green. You pick…?" — choice first, name second.\n3) Scribble side by side in silence for a minute. Companionable noise from the crayons only.\n4) Ask the golden question about THEIR page: "tell me about your drawing!" Listen. Follow the story wherever it goes — cars, monsters, grandma.\n5) Date and display: pin it up, or fridge-front it. An audience keeps authors writing.',
  say:'"Crayons! Let\u2019s draw. Pick a crayon… I have green. Scribble, scribble! Look at the colors. Now — tell me about your drawing. Oh! It\u2019s a car going to grandma\u2019s? Tell me more!"',
  notice:'On this card the word "crayons" sits under five crayons standing in a yellow-and-green box — the toolkit, packed and ready.',
  ask:'"Which color is that?" "Tell me about your drawing!" "Can you make a big circle? A fast line?"',
  childSay:'Your child might name colors while scribbling, narrate the story ("it\u2019s a dog… running!"), or hand you a crayon and demand you draw — all of it is authorship.',
  real:'Color and mark live everywhere: the menu at the restaurant, chalk on the pavement, the shopping list. "I need my crayons for THAT!" is the sound of the word working.',
  play:'Story Gallery: three drawings, pinned in a row. A tour guide (the artist) walks you past each one and tells its story. Tickets are optional; pride is not.',
  check:'Ask: "What are these?" (crayons) "Which color?" "Tell me about your picture!" — name, choose, narrate: the writer\u2019s first lesson is done.'},
 crib:{
  title:'The Crib Lesson',one:'Teach the bed word as the last line of the bedtime script — rehearsed by teddy first.',
  seoTitle:'Crib Lesson for Toddlers — The Bed Word at Bedtime (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 1–3: how to teach "crib" with a teddy-goes-to-bed rehearsal and a same-sentence script for the crib rail.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "crib" through a rehearsal: teddy goes to bed first, the same sentence lands at the rail every night, and the bed word becomes the period at the end of the day.',
  what:'The crib is the toddler\u2019s own bed — bars, mattress, the yellow blanket — and the word that ends every day when it is said in the same place at the same time.',
  mean:'"Crib" is a place word with a promise inside: sleep is next. Toddlers who can name the destination fight the journey less.',
  know:'You need the crib, one sentence and a teddy with a bed of his own (a shoebox works). The sentence — "into the crib, night-night" — is said at the rail, every night, until the child says it first.',
  why:'Rituals are how toddlers tell time. The crib sentence is the last line of the day\u2019s script; a known last line is what lets a small body let go.',
  dev:'Routine vocabulary, day-night sequencing, gentle transfer (the teddy tuck), and the self-settling that a predictable bed invites.',
  vocab:'crib, bed, sleep, night-night, cozy, teddy',
  teach:'1) At play, introduce teddy\u2019s bed: "teddy is sleepy — into his crib! Night-night, teddy."\n2) Let the toddler do the tucking — sloppiness is love.\n3) Keep teddy\u2019s routine shorter than theirs: he sleeps NOW so the idea is fresh tonight.\n4) At the real bedtime, the same sentence at the rail: "into the crib, night-night." Same words, same spot.\n5) Night after night, drop your voice a little more each word — the sentence itself becomes the wind-down.',
  say:'"Crib! This is your crib — your very own bed. Cozy crib, soft blanket. Into the crib we go… night-night. Sleep tight. The crib is where sleep lives."',
  notice:'On this card the word "crib" sits under a blue crib with yellow bedding — the bed itself, made and waiting.',
  ask:'"Where do you sleep?" "Where does teddy sleep?" "What do we say at the crib?" (night-night!)',
  childSay:'Your child might say "nigh-nigh" at the rail, tuck teddy unprompted, or climb in clutching the blanket — the word and the routine shaking hands.',
  real:'Beds are everywhere in books and lives — the baby\u2019s crib, grandpa\u2019s big bed, the dog\u2019s basket. "Where does everyone sleep?" is a whole-car-ride game.',
  play:'Sleepy Zoo: every animal needs tucking into a "crib" (boxes, cushions, pots). The zookeeper tucks each one with the bedtime word. Lights out, zoo closed.',
  check:'Ask: "What is this?" (crib) "Where do YOU sleep?" "Say it at the rail… what do we say?" Bed, point, night-night — the day can end now.'},
 diaper:{
  title:'The Diaper Lesson',one:'Teach the most-practised routine word — with one job per change.',
  seoTitle:'Diaper Lesson for Toddlers — The Change-Time Word (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 1–3: how to teach "diaper" with a narrated change script and a helper job that turns changes into cooperation.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "diaper" by narrating the change in the same short words every time — and by giving the toddler one job, because busy hands do not protest.',
  what:'A diaper is the padded garment that gets changed many times a day — the most-repeated routine word in the toddler vocabulary by a mile.',
  mean:'The diaper word is the first link in a chain: diaper → potty → toilet. Naming it kindly now makes every later link sound friendlier.',
  know:'You need the change script (lie down… fresh diaper… all clean!) and one job per change: fetch the diaper, carry it to the bin, hold the cream, choose front or back. One is plenty.',
  why:'Changes happen a dozen times a day; that is a dozen vocabulary lessons and a dozen cooperation chances hiding in a routine everyone already does.',
  dev:'Body vocabulary, following a two-step instruction, carrying and bin-aiming (real hand-eye work), and potty-learning language seeded early.',
  vocab:'diaper, fresh, clean, lie down, bin, all done',
  teach:'1) Before the change: "diaper! Time for a fresh diaper."\n2) Send the helper: "fetch the diaper, please!" — the fetch IS the lesson.\n3) Narrate in threes: "lie down… fresh diaper… tabs click."\n4) Assign the send-off: the toddler carries the old diaper to the bin — aim, drop, lid down.\n5) Close every time with "ALL DONE, all clean!" — the fanfare makes the routine applaudable.',
  say:'"Diaper! Time for a fresh one. Fetch the diaper… thank you! Lie down… fresh diaper… tabs click… ALL DONE! Now take it to the bin — in it goes! All clean."',
  notice:'On this card the word "diaper" sits under a white taped diaper — the everyday object itself, plain as the routine it names.',
  ask:'"What do you need?" (a fresh diaper!) "Can you fetch the diaper?" "Where does the old one go?" (in the bin)',
  childSay:'Your child might say "diaper!" when wet, fetch it proudly, or slam the bin lid like a drum — the routine with sound effects.',
  real:'Diapers live in every bag and every shop. Spotting the pack at the store — "diapers! Our diapers!" — turns errands into vocabulary trips.',
  play:'Dolly\u2019s Day: the doll needs FIVE changes. The parent-in-training runs each one — fetch, change, bin, fanfare. Repetition is the lesson; the doll never complains.',
  check:'Ask: "What is this?" (diaper) "Fetch the diaper!" (a fetch) "Where does the old one go?" (bin) — name, fetch, aim: routine mastered.'},
 easel:{
  title:'The Easel Lesson',one:'Teach standing-up art — big arm circles now, pencil control later.',
  seoTitle:'Easel Lesson for Toddlers — Standing Art, Big Arms (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 2–3: how to teach "easel" with vertical scribbling — big arm circles, shoulder strength and one paper rule.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "easel" as the standing art station: paper clipped, arms up, big circles — the whole shoulder doing the work that writing will inherit.',
  what:'An easel is the standing frame that holds paper upright for drawing — here, the blue A-frame with the clip, at exactly standing height.',
  mean:'Vertical surfaces change the drawing: the shoulder leads, the wrist follows, and the big-muscle work now is the small-muscle precision later. The easel is pre-writing dressed as art.',
  know:'You need paper, the clip (or a fridge, or a big box wall) and the one rule: marks live on the paper. Everything else — color, subject, enthusiasm — belongs to the artist.',
  why:'High-energy toddlers focus better on their feet. The easel lets the body wiggle legally while the hands and eyes work — focus and movement, finally on the same side.',
  dev:'Shoulder and arm strength, vertical wrist position (the writing position), up-down and circle strokes, and the boundary discipline of one surface.',
  vocab:'easel, clip, paper, draw, circles, up high',
  teach:'1) Clip the paper together: "easel! Paper goes on — click."\n2) Stand side by side, crayons up: "big circles first!" Draw giant loops shoulder-high.\n3) Vary the direction: "up… down… round and round." Name the strokes as they happen.\n4) Trade surfaces: their half, your half — mirror each other\u2019s marks.\n5) The gallery finish: step back three steps, admire loudly, "shall we keep it or make another?" The artist decides.',
  say:'"Easel! Stand up tall — time to draw. Clip the paper… click! Big circles — round and round! Up, down, side to side… your arms are the engine. Look what you drew!"',
  notice:'On this card the word "easel" sits under a blue art easel holding a white sheet — the standing studio, ready for its first masterpiece.',
  ask:'"What is this?" (easel) "Draw a big circle!" "Where does the paper go?" (on the easel, clipped)',
  childSay:'Your child might say "I draw!", fill the page with loops, or clip the next sheet themselves — the studio is now self-serve.',
  real:'Standing art hides in plain sight: fridge galleries, chalk walls, the whiteboard at the clinic. Any vertical surface is an easel with good PR.',
  play:'Wall Mural: tape a big paper sheet to the wall, artists take turns, one rule (paper only). The mural grows all week — a standing monument to the easel word.',
  check:'Ask: "What is this?" (easel) "Where does the paper go?" (clipped on) "Show me a big circle!" — name, clip, circle: vertical art, unlocked.'},
 'floor-cushion':{
  title:'The Floor Cushion Lesson',one:'Teach the word for a spot of your own — and the flop that ends a big day.',
  seoTitle:'Floor Cushion Lesson for Toddlers — A Soft Spot of Your Own (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 2–3: how to teach "floor cushion" with the flop-and-sigh game and a reading spot that belongs to the child.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "floor cushion" as ownership and wind-down: this spot is YOURS, and the flop onto it — with the big sigh — is the game that practises calming down.',
  what:'A floor cushion is the round blue pad that lives on the floor — a toddler-sized seat that belongs to whoever flops on it.',
  mean:'A defined personal spot does quiet work: it answers "where do I fit?", gives big feelings a retreat with dignity, and gives grown-ups a non-sentence way to say "take a beat."',
  know:'You need the cushion parked somewhere consistent (book corner is ideal) and the flop ritual: stand, wobble, FLOP, sigh. The sigh is not decoration — it is slow breathing, taught by comedy.',
  why:'Calming down is a skill, not a temperament. Practised daily on a cushion, the flop-sigh becomes a tool the child can carry to any floor in the world.',
  dev:'Body regulation (the sigh), ownership vocabulary, sitting stamina for books, and the spatial word pair "on" and "off."',
  vocab:'floor cushion, my spot, sit, flop, sigh, cozy',
  teach:'1) Introduce the spot: "floor cushion! This is YOUR spot."\n2) Demonstrate the flop: stand, wobble like a sleepy robot, FLOP — "aaaaah." Exaggerate the sigh; comedy is the teacher.\n3) Offer the turn: the toddler\u2019s flop. Applaud the sigh especially.\n4) Park a book there: "your spot for stories." Read ON the cushion — the spot earns its job.\n5) On a stormy-feeling day, walk there together: "cushion time." No lecture, just the spot — the tool already knows what to do.',
  say:'"Floor cushion! Your very own soft spot. Watch me — wobble, wobble… FLOP! Aaaaah. Now you… FLOP! Big sigh! Cozy. This is where we read books and take soft landings."',
  notice:'On this card the words "floor cushion" sit under a round blue cushion — thick, soft and exactly the shape of a small sitting person\u2019s landing.',
  ask:'"Where is YOUR spot?" "Show me the flop!" "Where do we read books?" (on the cushion)',
  childSay:'Your child might say "my cushion!", flop dramatically on request, or drag a book there unprompted — ownership announced.',
  real:'Personal spots scale up: the reading corner at the library, the window seat at grandma\u2019s, the car seat that is "mine." Spots of one\u2019s own are a lifelong need, started on foam.',
  play:'Cushion Island: cushions on the floor are islands, the floor is water — hop, flop, read, repeat. The game ends the way islands should: everyone flopped, everyone sighing.',
  check:'Ask: "What is this?" (floor cushion) "Whose spot is it?" (mine!) "Show me the flop!" — name, own, flop: the soft spot is claimed.'},
 'high-chair':{
  title:'The High Chair Lesson',one:'Teach the mealtime seat word — said at the same moment every day.',
  seoTitle:'High Chair Lesson for Toddlers — The Seat Where Meals Happen (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 1–3: how to teach "high chair" with a climb-up ritual, a tray job before the food and meals that start smoother.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "high chair" as a word said on the walk to the kitchen — the same word, the same moment, every meal, so the sitting-down starts before the food does.',
  what:'The high chair is the tall toddler seat with the tray — where every meal happens, and where most mealtime battles are quietly won or lost before any food arrives.',
  mean:'The climb-in is the meal\u2019s first course. A named seat, a known climb and one tray job give the meal a calm opening — which the food then keeps.',
  know:'You need the word at the right moment (on the walk to the kitchen, never from across the house), a hand for the climb and one tray job before plates land: spoon placed, cup parked, bang delivered.',
  why:'Mealtime meltdowns are usually opening-scene meltdowns. A named, ritualised climb-in removes the surprise — and a toddler who chose to climb resists less than one who was placed.',
  dev:'Routine vocabulary, climbing control, tray-side object permanence (cup goes HERE), and the meal-start patience that eating cooperatively needs.',
  vocab:'high chair, climb, up, tray, sit, yummy',
  teach:'1) On the walk to the kitchen, the word: "high chair time!" — same tone, same moment, daily.\n2) The climb: offer a hand but let the leg-work be theirs. "Up you go — you did it!"\n3) The tray job before the food: "park your cup… click the spoon… ready!" A prepared tray is a prepared toddler.\n4) Eat beside them and name the good moments: "sitting tall — yum yum."\n5) The close: "all done!" — hands washed, cup to the sink. Meals that end on purpose start on purpose tomorrow.',
  say:'"High chair! Time to eat. Climb up… up you go! The tray clicks on — ready! Park your cup… there. Bang the spoon — hello, spoon! Now… yummy time."',
  notice:'On this card the words "high chair" sit under a blue high chair with a yellow tray — the seat itself, tray open for business.',
  ask:'"Where do you sit for meals?" "Can you climb up?" "Where does your cup go?" (on the tray)',
  childSay:'Your child might say "chair!" at the kitchen door, climb up half-alone, or park the cup exactly on the spot — the routine, self-run.',
  real:'Eating seats are everywhere: the café high chair, the booster at grandma\u2019s, the picnic bench. "Time for your eating chair!" keeps the rule traveling.',
  play:'Restaurant Night: the high chair is Table One, the teddies have Table Two. Menus (pictures), orders taken, trays prepared by the head waiter. Meals begin better when they are staged.',
  check:'Ask: "Where do meals happen?" (high chair) "Show me climb up!" "Where does the cup park?" — word, climb, park: seated and smiling.'},
 'laundry-basket':{
  title:'The Laundry Basket Lesson',one:'Teach the container word with the in-out game that hides a real chore.',
  seoTitle:'Laundry Basket Lesson for Toddlers — In, Out, In Again (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 2–3: how to teach "laundry basket" with a one-piece fetch game that is secretly tidying up, plus the peekaboo finale.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "laundry basket" through the in-and-out game toddlers are already obsessed with — and hides a real chore inside it, one garment at a time.',
  what:'A laundry basket is the woven container that holds the clothes — and, in this room, occasionally one giggling toddler.',
  mean:'Containers are toddler physics 101: in, out, full, empty. The basket card meets the obsession head-on and borrows its momentum for tidying.',
  know:'You need the basket, a small pile of laundry on the floor and the one-piece rule: fetch ONE thing, drop it in, say what it was — "socks IN!" Repetition is the engine; the peekaboo is the dessert.',
  why:'Chores at two are not chores — they are games with a finish line. The one-piece fetch is the smallest honest version of laundry day, and it counts.',
  dev:'In-out spatial concepts, carrying and dropping (aim!), item naming, following a repeated instruction, and the earliest work pride there is.',
  vocab:'laundry basket, in, out, fetch, socks, full',
  teach:'1) Present the basket: "laundry basket! The clothes live here."\n2) Model one fetch: "socks — IN!" Drop with ceremony.\n3) Hand over the job: "your turn — fetch ONE thing!" One at a time; the count is the game.\n4) Name every landing: "shirt in! Towel in!" The basket fills; the vocabulary does too.\n5) The finale: "is anything ELSE left?" — and the toddler, if willing, climbs in. "IN the basket! Peekaboo!" Empty basket, full victory.',
  say:'"Laundry basket! The clothes go IN. Fetch one thing… the socks! Socks IN. Now the shirt… shirt IN. Towel IN! Look — the basket is FULL. And where is YOU? In the basket! Peekaboo!"',
  notice:'On this card the words "laundry basket" sit under a blue woven basket — handles up, ready to hold the whole week\u2019s wash.',
  ask:'"Where do the clothes go?" "Fetch ONE thing!" "Is the basket full or empty?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say "IN!" on every drop, fetch items nobody asked for (enthusiasm!), or climb in before being asked — the schema demands it.',
  real:'Baskets live in every room: toy baskets, market baskets, the basket at the till. "That goes IN the basket!" scales the word to the whole house.',
  play:'Basket Post: the basket becomes a letterbox — toys "post" socks and clothes through the handles. Deliveries all afternoon; the laundry happens on schedule.',
  check:'Ask: "What is this?" (laundry basket) "Put the socks IN!" "Full or empty?" — name, drop, fill: the container word is in.'},
 'night-light':{
  title:'The Night Light Lesson',one:'Teach the little light that guards the dark — switched on by the child.',
  seoTitle:'Night Light Lesson for Toddlers — The Light That Guards (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 1–3: how to teach "night light" with a switch ritual the child owns and a goodnight-to-the-light close.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "night light" by handing over the switch: the child turns the dark friendly themselves, which is the whole trick.',
  what:'A night light is the small glowing lamp that stays on through the night — the friendly face a dark room makes when a toddler is in it.',
  mean:'Dark-room fears bloom around two. The night light works because it hands over agency: the child flicks the switch, the child names the light, the dark becomes managed — not banished, managed.',
  know:'You need the light at toddler-height reach and the ritual order: bath, pajamas, THEN the click. The click is the child\u2019s job; the "night-night, night light" is the close.',
  why:'A light the grown-ups sneak in later is a patch; a light the child switches on is a tool they run. Agency converts fear into competence — one click at a time.',
  dev:'Cause and effect (click, glow), routine sequencing, self-soothing with a named tool, and the confidence of operating something real.',
  vocab:'night light, dark, glow, click, on, night-night',
  teach:'1) At bedtime, present the switch: "night light! Your job — the click."\n2) Guide the finger, then fade the guide: click — "ON comes the night light!"\n3) Name what changed: "the room is dark BUT the night light glows. Yellow and soft."\n4) Say goodnight to it by name: "night-night, night light. Watch over me."\n5) Repeat the ritual identically nightly — sameness is what makes the click powerful.',
  say:'"Night light! Time for your job. Click… ON! Look — the room is dark, but the night light glows. Soft and yellow. It watches over you all night. Night-night, night light!"',
  notice:'On this card the words "night light" sit under a glowing plug-in lamp, light spilling yellow around it — the dark room\u2019s one friendly face.',
  ask:'"Who turns on the night light?" (you do!) "What color does it glow?" "What do we say to the night light?" (night-night!)',
  childSay:'Your child might say "I click it!", announce "light ON!" at dusk, or wave goodnight to the glow — agency in action.',
  real:'Lights that guard live everywhere: the hallway night light at grandma\u2019s, the hotel bathroom glow, the porch light. "A night light!" — the tool travels.',
  play:'Glow Hunt: dim the room in daytime play and find all the soft glows — night light, clock, phone (from afar). The dark gets mapped; mapped things stop being scary.',
  check:'Ask: "Who clicks the night light?" (me!) "What does it do?" (glows) "Say goodnight to it…" — click, glow, goodnight: the dark is staffed.'},
 pajamas:{
  title:'The Pajamas Lesson',one:'Teach the sleepy-clothes word — with the zip as the child\u2019s unmissable job.',
  seoTitle:'Pajamas Lesson for Toddlers — The Sleepy Clothes (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 1–3: how to teach "pajamas" with an arm-in-leg-in-zip script and the zip job the toddler never gives back.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "pajamas" through the dressing script — arm in, leg in, ZIP — and crowns the zip as the toddler\u2019s official job, tonight and forever.',
  what:'Pajamas are the one-piece sleep suit with the yellow zip — the official uniform of bedtime, and the second hand on the bedtime clock.',
  mean:'The pajama word is a signal: clothes, then teeth, then story, then crib. Same order nightly is what makes each step believable to a small body.',
  know:'You need the suit laid out and the three-part script said in the same order every night. The zip is non-negotiable child territory — guide the start, let the finish be theirs.',
  why:'A child who does the zip ends the dressing as the owner of it. Ownership at the zip leaks upward into ownership of the whole bedtime.',
  dev:'Dressing vocabulary (arm, leg, zip), two-hand coordination, sequencing, and the pride loop that makes tomorrow night easier.',
  vocab:'pajamas, sleepy clothes, arm in, leg in, zip, cozy',
  teach:'1) Lay the suit flat: "pajamas! The sleepy clothes."\n2) Script the arms: "arm in… other arm in!" — the child pushes limbs through where they can.\n3) Script the legs: "leg in… leg in… wiggles check!"\n4) Hand over the zip: "your job — the zip! Hold the bottom… up you go… ZIIIP!" (Guide the tab into the carter, then let them pull.)\n5) Admire: "cozy pajamas — bedtime team, dressed!" Straight into the next routine step so the sequence locks in.',
  say:'"Pajamas! The sleepy clothes. Arm in… leg in… now YOUR job — the zip! Hold it… pull… ZIIIP! All zipped — cozy pajamas! Bedtime team is dressed."',
  notice:'On this card the word "pajamas" sits under a blue one-piece suit with the yellow zip down the front — the uniform, standing to attention.',
  ask:'"What are these?" (pajamas!) "Where does the arm go?" "Whose job is the zip?" (mine!)',
  childSay:'Your child might say "zip!" with both hands ready, demand pajamas at the first yawn, or zip a jacket at breakfast for practice — the job is loved.',
  real:'Zips are everywhere: jackets, bags, tents. "Just like your pajama zip!" makes every zipper a practice rep for the big one.',
  play:'Dress the Teddy: teddy\u2019s "pajamas" (a sock with a hole, ceremonially) go on arm-in-leg-in while the toddler narrates. The zip is the finisher — teddy\u2019s or a bag\u2019s, any zip counts.',
  check:'Ask: "What are these?" (pajamas) "Show me arm in!" "Whose job is the zip?" — name, dress, zip: the bedtime team reports ready.'},
 'picture-book':{
  title:'The Picture Book Lesson',one:'Teach reading as pointing and naming — the tenth read beats the tenth new book.',
  seoTitle:'Picture Book Lesson for Toddlers — Point, Name, Again (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 1–3: how to teach "picture book" with point-and-name reading, the finger trail and why the same book ten times wins.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "picture book" as a pointing game: the child\u2019s finger finds, the grown-up names, the book gets read again — and again — and the tenth read is the best one.',
  what:'A picture book is a book whose pictures carry the story — here, the one with the teddy on the cover — and at this age reading IS the pointing game played on its pages.',
  mean:'Point-and-name builds vocabulary faster than any screen because the picture holds the meaning while the adult holds the voice. Repetition is not laziness — it is the curriculum.',
  know:'You need the book, a lap and the finger rule: the CHILD\u2019S finger does the finding. The adult\u2019s job is naming whatever the finger lands on — no agenda, no skipping ahead.',
  why:'The tenth read of the same book is when the child starts "reading" it from memory — turning pages, naming the bear, finishing the lines. That is real reading, months before letters.',
  dev:'Vocabulary by pointing, book handling (page turning is a skill), attention stamina, and the book-love that everything later stands on.',
  vocab:'picture book, book, point, page, read, again',
  teach:'1) Cover first: "picture book! Look — a bear on the cover. Hello, bear!"\n2) Open together; the child\u2019s finger leads: "where do you point?"\n3) Name what the finger finds: "a dog! A ball! A tree!" — no steering, just naming.\n4) Turn pages by the corner — let the page-turn be their skill, clumsy and cherished.\n5) The close: "finished… AGAIN?" Repeat at will. The tenth read is the goal, not the failure.',
  say:'"Picture book! Let\u2019s read. Open it up… point to something! Your finger says… a dog! Woof. Now a ball! Point again… What do you find? Read it again? Of course!"',
  notice:'On this card the words "picture book" sit under a book with a teddy bear on the cover under a sunny sky — a story waiting on the shelf\u2019s friendliest face.',
  ask:'"Where is the bear?" "Point to the dog!" "Turn the page…" "Read it again?"',
  childSay:'Your child might name a picture from memory, "read" aloud in babble-story, or bring the book as a request — reading, arriving early.',
  real:'Books live in every room and every bag. A book at breakfast, a book at the clinic: "picture book time!" The habit outruns the venue.',
  play:'Book Picnic: three books, one cushion, snacks for the grown-up. The child "reads" each to the audience — page turning, pointing, babbling narration. The critic\u2019s favourite: the one read forty times.',
  check:'Ask: "What is this?" (picture book) "Point to the bear!" "Turn the page!" — cover, point, turn: reading has begun.'},
 pillow:{
  title:'The Pillow Lesson',one:'Teach the head\u2019s landing spot — with the flop-sigh that practises falling asleep.',
  seoTitle:'Pillow Lesson for Toddlers — The Soft Landing (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 1–3: how to teach "pillow" with the wobble-flop-sigh game — slow breathing smuggled in as comedy at bedtime.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "pillow" through the flop: wobble like a sleepy robot, land on the pillow, sigh like the day is over — because it is.',
  what:'The pillow is the soft square at the head of the bed where the tired head lands — the last stop of every bedtime.',
  mean:'The flop-sigh is secret training: the exaggerated sigh slows the breathing, and slow breathing is the body\u2019s own sleep switch. Comedy first, physiology second.',
  know:'You need the pillow (on the bed, not the floor), a teddy for rehearsal and the three-beat game: wobble… flop… "aaaaah." The sigh is the star; coach it proudly.',
  why:'Toddlers cannot be ordered to relax, but they can be invited to perform a sigh. The game rehearses the exact exhale that falling asleep needs — nightly, joyfully, free.',
  dev:'Body regulation, head-neck control in the flop, routine vocabulary, and the muscle memory of exhale-and-settle.',
  vocab:'pillow, head, flop, sigh, soft, mmm',
  teach:'1) Daytime rehearsal: teddy flops first — "wobble, wobble… FLOP! aaaaah." The toddler attends to the comedy.\n2) Offer the turn: the toddler\u2019s flop onto the pillow. Coach the sigh: "bigger… aaaaah!"\n3) Name the landing: "your head on the pillow — mmm, soft."\n4) At bedtime, the same three beats, slower: wobble (a sleepy sway), flop (head down), sigh (long and soft).\n5) Fade the comedy, keep the sigh: within a week the flop IS the goodnight.',
  say:'"Pillow! The soft, squishy pillow. Wobble like a sleepy robot… flop! Your head lands… aaaaah. Mmm. The pillow holds your head all night. Goodnight, head!"',
  notice:'On this card the word "pillow" sits under a plump blue pillow — the square, soft landing every bedtime promises.',
  ask:'"Where does your head go?" (on the pillow) "Show me the flop!" "What does the sigh sound like?" (aaaaah)',
  childSay:'Your child might flop and sigh on request, demand "flop again!", or land mid-yawn at bedtime — the tool, self-deploying.',
  real:'Pillows are universal: the sofa cushion, grandma\u2019s guest pillow, the pool lounger. Any soft landing is "a pillow moment" — sigh included.',
  play:'Pillow Mountain: every cushion in the house, one summit, flops all round. The expedition ends flat, sighing, gloriously done.',
  check:'Ask: "What is this?" (pillow) "Where does the head land?" "Show me the sigh!" — name, flop, exhale: sleep is approaching.'},
 'play-mat':{
  title:'The Play Mat Lesson',one:'Teach the word that starts and ends playtime — unroll to begin, roll to finish.',
  seoTitle:'Play Mat Lesson for Toddlers — Where Play Lives (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 1–3: how to teach "play mat" with the unroll opening whistle and the roll-up finish that makes tidy-up visible.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "play mat" as playtime\u2019s frame: unrolling is the starting whistle, toys live on the mat, and the roll-up is the finish line everyone can see.',
  what:'The play mat is the rolled blue pad that unrolls into the floor\u2019s play surface — the stage every toy performs on.',
  mean:'A defined play space solves sprawl: toys stay in the kingdom, tidy-up has a border, and the words "on" and "off" get a home with edges.',
  know:'You need the mat and the two rituals: the unroll ("tadaaa! play time!") and the roll-up ("toys home… roll, roll… all done!"). Both belong to the child as soon as hands allow.',
  why:'Beginnings and endings are what toddlers need most. A mat that visibly starts and stops play gives the day\u2019s chaos a shape — and shape is calm.',
  dev:'Spatial words (on, off, edge), rolling motor skills, sequencing (open, play, close), and boundary respect that transfers to every surface in life.',
  vocab:'play mat, roll, unroll, on the mat, tidy, done',
  teach:'1) The opening ceremony: the child unrolls — "tadaaa! The play mat! Play time!"\n2) Set the kingdom\u2019s law gently: "toys live ON the mat." Scatter a few on purpose and ask: on or off?\n3) Play. The mat does its silent work while everyone forgets it is there.\n4) The finish: "toys go home on the mat… roll, roll, roll — ALL DONE!" The roll-up is the ending the day needs.\n5) Park the rolled mat in its spot: "the mat sleeps here. See you next play!"',
  say:'"Play mat! Roll it out — tadaaa! Play time! The toys go ON the mat… on the mat… on the mat. And when play is done: toys home, roll it up… all done! The mat sleeps."',
  notice:'On this card the words "play mat" sit under a rolled blue mat with a fold of it unrolled — mid-birth, on its way to being the floor\u2019s playground.',
  ask:'"Where do the toys live?" (on the mat) "Is the ball ON the mat or OFF?" "What do we do at the end?" (roll it up!)',
  childSay:'Your child might say "on the mat!" while tidying, unroll it solo with a tadaaa, or roll toys INTO the mat for fun — the border, understood.',
  real:'Mats scale up: picnic blankets, beach towels, gym mats. "Our play mat!" generalizes the kingdom to the park, the pool, the lawn.',
  play:'Mat Islands: cushions and mats across the floor are islands in the "sea" (the bare floor). Cross only by mat-hops. The border IS the game.',
  check:'Ask: "What is this?" (play mat) "Where do toys go?" (on the mat) "Show me roll it up!" — unroll, play, roll: playtime framed.'},
 potty:{
  title:'The Potty Lesson',one:'Teach the potty word long before training — the word arrives first, calmly.',
  seoTitle:'Potty Lesson for Toddlers — The Word Before the Training (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 2–3: how to teach "potty" months before training — name it, sit the teddy on it, and let the word make the tool an old friend.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "potty" early and casually — the word meets the child long before the training does, so the little chair is an old friend by the time it matters.',
  what:'The potty is the toddler\u2019s own little toilet — the blue chair with the yellow seat — and the word that the whole transition will be built on.',
  mean:'Potty learning is a language project before it is a skill project: children need the words (potty, pee, poo, flush) long before the timing clicks. Fear shrinks when naming comes first.',
  know:'You need the potty visible and casual — in the bathroom, never a surprise — and the teddy demonstration. NO pressure, NO performance: the card\u2019s job this week is just the word.',
  why:'Families who name the potty early report smoother training: the tool is familiar, the words are shared, and the child walks in curious instead of ambushed.',
  dev:'Body-awareness vocabulary, imitation (the teddy sits), routine sequencing (pants down, sit, wipe, flush), and the patience that training will test.',
  vocab:'potty, sit, pee, poo, flush, big kid',
  teach:'1) Show and name, zero drama: "potty! This is YOUR potty — your very own little chair."\n2) The teddy demo: teddy "sits" on the potty. "Teddy is trying! Well done, teddy."\n3) Invite a clothed sit: the toddler tries it, pants up, for the novelty. "You sit on the potty!"\n4) Seed the words at every change: "one day, pee and poo will go IN the potty — big kid stuff!"\n5) Leave the door open, literally: the potty stays visible, unremarkable, ready. The word keeps working between lessons.',
  say:'"Potty! This is the potty — your little chair. Teddy is sitting… well done, teddy! Want to try? Pants up is fine — just sitting. One day, pee and poo go in the potty. Big kid stuff!"',
  notice:'On this card the word "potty" sits under a blue potty chair with a yellow seat — small, sturdy and exactly child-sized.',
  ask:'"What is this?" (potty!) "Where does teddy sit?" "Who has a potty?" (you do!)',
  childSay:'Your child might announce "my potty!", sit the teddy repeatedly, or request a clothed sit at odd hours — curiosity, correctly aimed.',
  real:'Toilets are everywhere in a child\u2019s world: at home, at grandma\u2019s, the little cubby at the café. Each one is "a potty for big people" — the family of the word.',
  play:'Potty School: every doll and bear sits on the potty in turn while the toddler-teachers narrate: "sit… wait… well done!" The classroom runs itself.',
  check:'Ask: "What is this?" (potty) "Whose potty is it?" (mine) "Where does teddy sit?" — name, own, demo: the word is in place before the training is.'},
 puzzle:{
  title:'The Puzzle Lesson',one:'Teach the try-again skill — one piece at a time, narrated calmly.',
  seoTitle:'Puzzle Lesson for Toddlers — Turn, Try, Click (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 2–3: how to teach "puzzle" with one-piece handovers, narrated trying and the calm try-again that is the real lesson.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "puzzle" through the handover: one piece at a time, the trying narrated out loud, and the CLICK celebrated as what it is — persistence paying off.',
  what:'The puzzle is the four-piece wooden board — four bright pieces, four matching holes, and the toddler\u2019s first lab of matching and patience.',
  mean:'Puzzles teach the fail-again muscle: a piece that will not go in is not broken, it is turned wrong. The grown-up\u2019s calm voice while trying is the actual curriculum.',
  know:'You need the board, the pieces in YOUR hand (one at a time) and the narration: "turn it… flip it… try again… it FITS!" Never grab the piece; the struggle is the school.',
  why:'Four pieces is victory-sized: enough challenge to demand trying, not enough to flood. Finishing builds the identity — "I am someone who finishes" — worth more than the skill itself.',
  dev:'Shape matching, wrist rotation, visual scanning, frustration tolerance, and the one-to-one logic of piece-into-hole.',
  vocab:'puzzle, piece, turn, try again, fits, click',
  teach:'1) Empty the board together: "puzzle! Four pieces, four spots."\n2) Hand over ONE piece: "try it… turn it around… CLICK! It fits! Red in red."\n3) The next piece resists: narrate the calm: "hmm… turn it… FLIP it… try again… CLICK!"\n4) Fade your words as the trying improves — the child inherits the narration.\n5) The finish: lift the whole board and pour the pieces out — "AGAIN!" The second round runs smoother than the first; that is learning, visible.',
  say:'"Puzzle! Four pieces, four spots. Here is one — try it… turn it… click! It fits! This one is tricky… turn… flip… try again… CLICK! All four — finished! Puzzle champions!"',
  notice:'On this card the word "puzzle" sits under a four-piece wooden board in red, yellow, green and blue — every piece out, every hole waiting.',
  ask:'"Where does the red piece go?" "Turn it… now what?" "What do we say when it does not fit?" (try again!)',
  childSay:'Your child might say "click!" on every fit, narrate their own trying, or pour the pieces out unprompted — persistence with a sound effect.',
  real:'Fitting lives everywhere: keys in doors, toasters, shape sorters, the last piece of toast in the bag. "It fits — like your puzzle!" keeps the skill hunting.',
  play:'Mystery Piece: hide one piece under a cup while the child closes their eyes — which piece is missing? Find it, place it, repeat. Deduction joins the matching.',
  check:'Ask: "What is this?" (puzzle) "Show me try again!" "What happens when it clicks?" (it fits!) — try, flip, click: persistence installed.'},
 shoes:{
  title:'The Shoes Lesson',one:'Teach the word that means adventure — and the foot-in job that starts it.',
  seoTitle:'Shoes Lesson for Toddlers — Shoes On, Adventure On (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 1–3: how to teach "shoes" with the foot-in ceremony at the door and the Velcro rip that makes the child the finisher.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "shoes" at the door, where the word works: foot in, foot in, and the Velcro rip that is the toddler\u2019s signature on getting dressed.',
  what:'Shoes are the blue-and-yellow sneakers that mean OUTSIDE — the most exciting dressing word in the language, because it always predicts adventure.',
  mean:'The shoe word works because its reward never varies: shoes on, we go out. A word with a perfect track record gets believed — and said — early.',
  know:'You need the shoes, a sitting spot by the door and the ceremony: present each shoe, foot in, then hand over the Velcro. The rip is the child\u2019s job; never do it for them.',
  why:'Dressing battles shrink when the child owns the finisher. The Velcro rip is tiny, loud and theirs — the exact size of job that makes a two-year-old invincible.',
  dev:'Foot-eye coordination (the aim into the shoe), grip strength for the rip, left-right body awareness, and dressing-sequence pride.',
  vocab:'shoes, foot, Velcro, rip, on, outside',
  teach:'1) By the door, the word: "shoes! Shoes on means we go OUT!"\n2) The ceremony: present the first shoe like a crown — "one foot… IN!"\n3) Second shoe, their push: "other foot… push… THERE!"\n4) Hand over the rip: "your job — rip the Velcro! RIIIIP!" Both shoes, both rips, both celebrations.\n5) Straight out the door: the word\u2019s reward arrives on schedule, every time, which is why it never needs teaching twice.',
  say:'"Shoes! Where are your shoes? There they are! One foot — in! Other foot — push! Now YOUR job… rip the Velcro! RIIIIP! Shoes on… let\u2019s go OUTSIDE!"',
  notice:'On this card the word "shoes" sits under a pair of blue and yellow sneakers — laced, waiting, facing the door.',
  ask:'"Where are your shoes?" "Which foot goes in first?" "Whose job is the Velcro?" (mine!)',
  childSay:'Your child might say "shoes!" at the door, race to sit on the step, or rip the Velcro with a war cry — adventure, formally opened.',
  real:'Shoes live in racks, by doors, under beds — and on everyone else. "Look, daddy\u2019s shoes! Baby\u2019s shoes!" is a matching game the whole family plays.',
  play:'Shoe Shop: every shoe in the house on display, the toddler is the shopkeeper — "trying on" each pair (one foot only is fine), ripping every strap in stock. Fittings daily.',
  check:'Ask: "What are these?" (shoes) "Where do feet go?" (in the shoes) "Whose job is the rip?" — name, aim, rip: out the door.'},
 'sippy-cup':{
  title:'The Sippy Cup Lesson',one:'Teach the big-kid cup — two hands, one sip, parked in its spot.',
  seoTitle:'Sippy Cup Lesson for Toddlers — Two Hands, One Sip (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 1–3: how to teach "sippy cup" with the two-hands script, the park-it finish and the independence that pours from it.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "sippy cup" with the two-hand lift — the child\u2019s first owned drink — and the park-it finish that gives the cup (and the meal) an ending.',
  what:'The sippy cup is the blue cup with the green lid and two handles — the bridge between bottle and open cup, and the first drink the child runs alone.',
  mean:'Drinking is a full skill stack: grip, lift, tip, swallow, park. Naming the cup claims it — MY cup — and ownership is what makes practice self-driven.',
  know:'You need the cup at every meal and the script: "two hands… lift… sip sip sip… put it down." The park is real: the cup has a spot, and parking it ends the sip.',
  why:'Two hands before one: the two-hand grip is the training wheels of drinking. Mastered here, the open cup arrives months earlier — and the floor stays drier.',
  dev:'Two-hand grip and lift, tip control (the difference between a sip and a shower), swallowing coordination, and object-parking (a place for everything).',
  vocab:'sippy cup, two hands, lift, sip, put down, my cup',
  teach:'1) Present at mealtime: "sippy cup! YOUR cup."\n2) Script the lift: "two hands… one, two… LIFT… sip sip sip."\n3) Coach the tip small: "tiny tips — just a little! …perfect."\n4) The park: "all done sipping? Put it DOWN — in its spot." Point to the spot; parking becomes automatic.\n5) Fade the script word by word over weeks until "cup!" triggers the whole routine solo.',
  say:'"Sippy cup! Here is YOUR cup. Two hands… lift… sip sip sip. Tiny tips! …All done? Put it down — park it in its spot. Good drinking, my friend!"',
  notice:'On this card the words "sippy cup" sit under a blue cup with a green lid and two handles — gripped exactly the way two small hands will grip it.',
  ask:'"Where is your cup?" "How many hands?" (two!) "Where does the cup go after?" (in its spot)',
  childSay:'Your child might say "my cup!", lift with exaggerated two-handed care, or park it unprompted — independence, one sip at a time.',
  real:'Cups are everywhere: the café\u2019s lid cup, grandma\u2019s special cup, the big people\u2019s mugs. "Someday, a cup like that — first, master THIS one." The ladder is visible.',
  play:'Cup Picnic: every teddy has a cup, the toddler serves each one — "teddy\u2019s cup… bunny\u2019s cup!" — then drinks their own with the two-hand script. Hosting and hydration, together.',
  check:'Ask: "What is this?" (sippy cup) "How many hands lift it?" (two) "Where does it park?" — grip, sip, park: the drink is owned.'},
 soap:{
  title:'The Soap Lesson',one:'Teach hand-washing as a named ceremony — wet, soap, rub, rinse, done.',
  seoTitle:'Soap Lesson for Toddlers — Bubbles and Clean Hands (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 1–3: how to teach "soap" with the five-step sink ceremony — and why the same order every time makes it automatic.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "soap" inside the sink ceremony: water on, soap on, rub the fronts, rub the backs, rinse — the same five beats every wash, until the hands do it themselves.',
  what:'Soap is the pink bar (or the pump) that makes the bubbles that take the germs away — the hero of the first hygiene habit a child ever owns.',
  mean:'Hand-washing is the most reusable routine in the whole set: it travels from this sink to every sink for life. The ceremony structure — same order, sung — is what makes it stick.',
  know:'You need the sink, the soap at reach and the five-beat script, sung or chanted: "wet… SOAP… rub the fronts… rub the backs… RINSE!" The child\u2019s jobs: pressing the pump, the rubbing, the rinsing.',
  why:'Germs are invisible, but bubbles are not. The bubbles give the child proof of work — "look, soap is ON!" — which is why the soap step can never be skipped.',
  dev:'Sequencing (five steps in order), rubbing strength and coverage, water-temperature respect (grown-up checks), and the lifetime habit itself.',
  vocab:'soap, bubbles, wash, rub, rinse, all clean',
  teach:'1) Walk to the sink naming the destination: "soap time! Hands get washed here."\n2) Beat one, water: "wet the hands… woosh."\n3) Beat two, the star: "SOAP! Press the pump… look — bubbles!" (Or rub the pink bar.)\n4) Beats three and four: "rub the fronts… rub the backs… between the fingers!" Twenty seconds, sung.\n5) Beat five, the close: "RINSE! Watch the bubbles go… ALL CLEAN!" Towel, fanfare, exit — the ceremony owns the germs.',
  say:'"Soap! Time to wash. Water on — wet hands. SOAP — press! Bubbles! Rub the fronts… rub the backs… between the fingers… RINSE! The bubbles take the germs away. ALL CLEAN — sparkly hands!"',
  notice:'On this card the word "soap" sits under a pink bar of soap — smooth, plain and exactly as bossy as a good hygiene hero should be.',
  ask:'"What makes the bubbles?" (soap!) "Where do we wash?" (at the sink) "What do we say at the end?" (all clean!)',
  childSay:'Your child might say "soap!" at every sink sighted, pump twice (enthusiasm), or sing the rub song solo — the ceremony, self-run.',
  real:'Sinks follow children everywhere: restaurants, clinics, airports. The ceremony travels with them — five beats, any basin, germs lose.',
  play:'Doll Wash Day: the dolls\u2019 hands need washing too! The toddler runs the full five beats for each customer. Standards are high; bubbles are mandatory.',
  check:'Ask: "What makes bubbles?" (soap) "Show me rub the fronts!" "What comes after rinse?" (all clean!) — press, rub, rinse: the habit is built.'},
 socks:{
  title:'The Socks Lesson',one:'Teach the first self-dressing skill — over the toes, pull, up!',
  seoTitle:'Socks Lesson for Toddlers — One Foot, Two Socks (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 1–3: how to teach "socks" with the over-the-toes pull-up — the first garment most toddlers ever put on themselves.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "socks" as the gateway dressing skill: stretchy, forgiving, and the first garment a toddler can genuinely put on alone — with cheers.',
  what:'Socks are the pair of blue foot-covers that come before shoes — stretchy, matchable and perfectly designed for small hands to learn on.',
  mean:'The sock teaches two ideas at once: the dressing pull (over toes, up the heel) and the pair concept — two the same, one per foot. Matching and dressing in one tiny garment.',
  know:'You need the pair and the start position: YOU begin the sock over the toes, the child does the pulling-up. The heel-hitch can wait; the pull is tonight\u2019s win.',
  why:'Self-dressing starts where resistance is lowest. A sock pulled up by its owner is a two-minute win that funds the courage for shirts, pants and zips later.',
  dev:'Pinch-grip and pull, over-the-toes coordination, pair matching (find the twin!), and the left-right body mapping that dressing quietly builds.',
  vocab:'socks, toes, pull, up, pair, cozy toes',
  teach:'1) Present the pair: "socks! Two the same — a pair!"\n2) Start one sock over the toes: "over the toes… now YOU — pull, pull, UP!"\n3) The heel is optional: "almost… there! Cozy toes!" Progress over precision.\n4) The hunt: "where is the other sock? Find the twin!" Matching under the bed is half the lesson.\n5) Repeat on the second foot — then the mirror check: "TWO socks — you dressed your feet!"',
  say:'"Socks! One sock… over the toes… pull, pull… UP! Now the other sock — find the twin! There it is! Over the toes… pull… UP. Two socks — cozy toes! You did your feet yourself!"',
  notice:'On this card the word "socks" sits under a pair of blue socks — heel to toe, folded the way pairs wait in the drawer.',
  ask:'"Where are the socks?" "Find the other sock!" "Who pulled it up?" (me!)',
  childSay:'Your child might say "socks on!", hunt the twin with detective energy, or pull socks onto hands as mittens (innovation) — dressing, claimed.',
  real:'Sock pairs run the whole house: laundry lines, drawer sortings, the mysterious single sock. "Find its pair!" is a chore-seed and a matching drill in one.',
  play:'Sock Match-Up: six socks, three pairs, mixed on the floor. Match them all, then dress the teddy in the loosest pair. Sorting, pairing, grooming — one basket of socks.',
  check:'Ask: "What are these?" (socks) "Where do they go?" (on the feet) "Show me pull UP!" — over toes, pull, twin: feet dressed by their owner.'},
 'stacking-rings':{
  title:'The Stacking Rings Lesson',one:'Teach size order — the big ring simply will not sit on top.',
  seoTitle:'Stacking Rings Lesson for Toddlers — Big to Small (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 2–3: how to teach "stacking rings" with the size-order discovery — the toy itself teaches biggest-first, no words needed.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "stacking rings" by letting the toy do the teaching: the big ring will not sit on the small ring, and the discovery is the lesson.',
  what:'The stacking rings are the rainbow stacker on its yellow post — five rings from biggest to smallest, and the size rule hiding inside every try.',
  mean:'Ordering by size is first maths. The stacker teaches it the best way possible — physically: wrong order simply does not work, so the hands discover what words would take weeks to explain.',
  know:'You need the stacker and the discipline NOT to correct: hand the rings in random order, let the tries happen, narrate only the wins. The post is the patient teacher.',
  why:'Children remember what they discover. A ring that refuses to stack teaches "big at the bottom" more durably than any instruction — and the joy is undamaged because nobody failed.',
  dev:'Size comparison (big, bigger, small), trial-and-error problem-solving, posting accuracy (ring to peg), and color words riding along free.',
  vocab:'stacking rings, big, small, biggest, first, rainbow',
  teach:'1) Empty the post: "stacking rings! Where do they go — ON the post!"\n2) Hand rings in mixed sizes. Say nothing about order; watch the experiments.\n3) Narrate the discovery when it happens: "the BIG ring — whoosh, down to the bottom!"\n4) Name the wins as the tower grows: "red… green… yellow… and the teeny tiny one — TOP!"\n5) The remix: unstack and line the rings up on the floor, biggest to smallest — a rainbow snake. Same lesson, new shape.',
  say:'"Stacking rings! Ring on the post… ooh, that one will not go! Try the BIG one first — whoosh, down it goes! Now the next… and the next… teeny tiny on top! A rainbow tower!"',
  notice:'On this card the words "stacking rings" sit under the rainbow stacker on its yellow post — every ring off, the tower waiting to happen.',
  ask:'"Which ring is the biggest?" "Where does the big ring go?" (bottom!) "Which one goes on top?" (the tiny one)',
  childSay:'Your child might announce "big one FIRST!", swap a wrong ring without drama, or roll the rings across the floor (physics is also on the syllabus) — ordering, discovered.',
  real:'Size ordering is everywhere: nesting cups, the Russian dolls at grandma\u2019s, plates in the cupboard. "Big to small — like your rings!" finds the maths hiding in kitchens.',
  play:'Ring Race: two players, two posts, same pile — who builds the rainbow first? (Nobody loses; both towers are celebrated, then knocked down with ceremony.)',
  check:'Ask: "Which is the biggest?" "Show me the tiny one!" "Where does big go?" (first — the bottom!) — size, order, tower: maths in colors.'},
 stroller:{
  title:'The Stroller Lesson',one:'Teach the word for little legs\u2019 vehicle — climb in, click, off we go.',
  seoTitle:'Stroller Lesson for Toddlers — The Ride for Little Legs (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 2–3: how to teach "stroller" with the climb-in-click ritual and a push-the-teddy rehearsal that makes outings smoother.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "stroller" as the family vehicle: the climb is the child\u2019s job, the buckle\u2019s click is the finisher, and teddy\u2019s stroller rehearses the whole ride.',
  what:'The stroller is the blue pushchair by the door — the vehicle that carries little legs when little legs are done, and the routine word that makes leaving the park survivable.',
  mean:'The stroller word lives where friction lives: getting in, getting out, leaving the fun. A named climb-in-click script turns the two worst moments of any outing into known steps.',
  know:'You need the stroller, the climb (child-powered with a hand nearby) and the click: the final buckle snap belongs to the child. Then the teddy stroller for rehearsal at home.',
  why:'A child who climbs in themselves and clicks themselves is a passenger with ownership — and passengers with ownership disembark with dignity when the ride is over.',
  dev:'Climbing control, buckle manipulation (fine motor), leaving-the-fun transitions, and the pushing-the-teddy empathy loop.',
  vocab:'stroller, climb in, buckle, click, push, off we go',
  teach:'1) At the stroller: "stroller! Time to climb in."\n2) The child-powered climb — hand hovering, not hauling: "up you go!"\n3) Buckle together, child finishes: "click! Ready to roll."\n4) At home, the rehearsal: teddy rides the toy stroller — "push teddy… teddy is safe and cozy!" The driver learns what passengers feel.\n5) On outings, the same two words every time: "stroller — climb in!" and "stroller — click!" Sameness is the secret.',
  say:'"Stroller! Climb in — up you go! Buckle the straps… and CLICK! You did it. Off we go — wheels go round! And when the ride is done, click again — you hop out, big and safe."',
  notice:'On this card the word "stroller" sits under a blue pushchair — seat back, wheels ready, parked exactly where the outing begins.',
  ask:'"Where do you ride?" (the stroller) "Who clicks the buckle?" (me!) "Where does teddy sit?" (in HIS stroller)',
  childSay:'Your child might say "stroller!" at the park gate, climb and click unprompted, or push teddy on serious rounds — the vehicle, fully staffed.',
  real:'Strollers park outside shops, fold into cars, queue at airports. Every sighting is a name-check: "stroller — like yours!" The word rides along.',
  play:'Teddy Bus Route: the toy stroller does the circuit — teddy on at the sofa stop, off at the kitchen stop, next passenger please. The toddler is driver, conductor and mechanic.',
  check:'Ask: "What is this?" (stroller) "Who clicks the buckle?" "Show me climb in!" — climb, click, ride: the family vehicle is run by its passenger.'},
 'stuffed-bunny':{
  title:'The Stuffed Bunny Lesson',one:'Teach body words on a friend — bunny\u2019s nose, then YOUR nose.',
  seoTitle:'Stuffed Bunny Lesson for Toddlers — Bunny\u2019s Nose, Your Nose (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 1–3: how to teach "stuffed bunny" with the find-the-part game that transfers body vocabulary from toy to child.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "stuffed bunny" through the find-the-part game: ears, nose, paws, tail — found on the bunny first, then on the child, which is how body words travel.',
  what:'The stuffed bunny is the cream plush rabbit with the long ears — the soft friend who will spend the next decade listening to everything.',
  mean:'A loved toy is the safest classroom: every part of the bunny can be named, touched and giggled over, then found again on the child\u2019s own body. The transfer is nearly automatic.',
  know:'You need the bunny and the two-step find: "where are bunny\u2019s EARS?" (point) "Where are YOUR ears?" (point). Four parts is a full lesson: ears, nose, paws, tail.',
  why:'Body vocabulary lands best on something the child adores — and lands twice as well when it hops onto their own body in the same breath.',
  dev:'Body-part vocabulary, pointing accuracy, gentle-touch practice, and the pretend-play seed: bunny will soon eat, sleep and get "poorly" on schedule.',
  vocab:'stuffed bunny, ears, nose, paws, tail, hop',
  teach:'1) Hello the friend: "stuffed bunny! Soft bunny — say hello!"\n2) Find on the bunny: "where are bunny\u2019s EARS? …There! Long, soft ears."\n3) Transfer: "and where are YOUR ears? …There! You have ears too!"\n4) Repeat for nose, paws (hands!), tail — bunny first, child second, every time.\n5) The finale: "bunny hops! Can YOU hop?" Two hops and a hug close the lesson — and open a decade of friendship.',
  say:'"Stuffed bunny! Soft bunny, long ears. Where are bunny\u2019s ears? …There! Where are YOUR ears? …There! Bunny\u2019s nose… YOUR nose! Bunny\u2019s paws… YOUR hands! Now — hop hop hop, little bunnies!"',
  notice:'On this card the words "stuffed bunny" sit under a cream plush rabbit — ears up, paws forward, the gentlest teacher in the room.',
  ask:'"Where are bunny\u2019s ears?" "Where are YOUR ears?" "How does a bunny move?" (hop!)',
  childSay:'Your child might say "bunny!" with a hug, point to their own nose proudly, or hop-hop across the room — vocabulary, hopping.',
  real:'Plush friends travel: in the car, to the clinic, to bed. Each carries the game — "bunny\u2019s nose… your nose!" — to every waiting room in town.',
  play:'Bunny School: the child teaches the bunny ITS body parts — "this is your nose, bunny!" Teaching reverses the game and doubles the vocabulary. Every pupil needs a name; the child supplies it.',
  check:'Ask: "Where are bunny\u2019s ears?" (point) "Where are YOUR ears?" (point) "Hop like bunny!" — find, transfer, hop: body words, delivered by a friend.'},
 'table-and-chair':{
  title:'The Table and Chair Lesson',one:'Teach the little workstation — sit, make, push the chair in.',
  seoTitle:'Table and Chair Lesson for Toddlers — The Little Work Spot (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 2–3: how to teach "table and chair" with the sit-and-make ritual and the chair push-in that signs every finished job.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "table and chair" as the toddler\u2019s workstation: activities happen here, and the push-in of the chair is the signature at the bottom of every finished job.',
  what:'The table and chair are the child-sized yellow table and its green-and-red seat — furniture built to toddler scale so little legs sit flat and little arms reach everything.',
  mean:'A defined workspace does what it does in any office: it tells the body "this is where work happens," and the push-in tells it the work is finished. Montessori\u2019s oldest trick, still undefeated.',
  know:'You need the table set for ONE activity at a time and the closing ritual: pieces in the box, crayons in the tub, chair pushed in. The push-in is always the child\u2019s.',
  why:'One activity out, one activity away gives the morning a rhythm the child can predict — and predictability is where focus comes from.',
  dev:'Sitting stamina, workspace respect, task completion (open-do-close), and the body mechanics of a proper seated reach.',
  vocab:'table, chair, sit, work, push in, all done',
  teach:'1) Invite to the seat: "table and chair! Sit on YOUR chair… scoot in."\n2) One activity, ready on the tabletop: "today the puzzle lives on the table."\n3) Work together, narrating lightly — the table is where focus lives.\n4) The finish line: "all done! Pieces in the box… crayons in the tub…"\n5) The signature: "push your chair in… CLICK-scoot. Job complete!" Admire the pushed-in chair like a signed painting.',
  say:'"Table and chair! This is your work spot. Sit on the chair… scoot in… the puzzle lives here today. We work… we finish… pieces in the box — and push your chair in! All done. What a worker!"',
  notice:'On this card the words "table and chair" sit under a small yellow table with a green and red chair — child-height, ready for one activity and no more.',
  ask:'"Where do we work?" (at the table) "Whose chair is this?" (mine!) "What do we do when we finish?" (push it in!)',
  childSay:'Your child might scoot in unasked, refuse all other chairs ("MY table!"), or push the chair in with a flourish — the workstation, staffed.',
  real:'Work tables scale: the kitchen table for baking, the desk at school one day, the picnic bench. "Your table!" generalizes the habit to every surface ahead.',
  play:'Café Table One: the table hosts a rotating menu — puzzle at ten, crayons at eleven, snack at noon. The maître d\u2019 (the toddler) seats every guest and pushes in every chair.',
  check:'Ask: "Where do we work?" (table) "What do we push when we finish?" (the chair) "Show me scoot in!" — sit, make, push: work has an address.'},
 'teddy-bear':{
  title:'The Teddy Bear Lesson',one:'Teach the classic cuddle — and let the teddy rehearse every routine first.',
  seoTitle:'Teddy Bear Lesson for Toddlers — The Classic Cuddle (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 1–3: how to teach "teddy bear" with a teddy-does-everything day — feeding, brushing, potty, bed — the rehearsal that smooths real routines.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "teddy bear" as the class pet: the teddy eats first, brushes first, sits first and sleeps first — rehearsing every routine in this set before the toddler\u2019s turn.',
  what:'The teddy bear is the brown plush bear with the round tummy — the classic first friend, dragged by one arm through every meal, nap and adventure.',
  mean:'The teddy is both comfort and curriculum: whatever the child does to the bear, the child is rehearsing. A teddy who eats, brushes and sleeps on schedule makes those routines normal, expected, done.',
  know:'You need the bear and the rehearsal plan: today teddy joins one routine FIRST — feeding, or teeth, or bed. The child plays parent; you narrate. Never force the teddy on the child; invite.',
  why:'Big feelings talk to bears more easily than to grown-ups, and new routines rehearse better on bears than on tired toddlers. The bear is the safest practice ground in the house.',
  dev:'Empathy and care-taking (the parenting instinct, born), routine sequencing, gentle-hand control, and the comfort-object bond that travels to bed and back.',
  vocab:'teddy bear, hug, feed, gentle, sleepy, night-night',
  teach:'1) Hello the bear: "teddy bear! Give him a gentle hug — squeeze!"\n2) Pick ONE routine for rehearsal: "teddy is hungry! Feed teddy his spoon."\n3) The child feeds; you narrate: "one bite for teddy… yummy! Now a drink."\n4) Extend gently: teddy\u2019s teeth get brushed, teddy sits on the potty, teddy gets his blanket — one per sitting, never all at once.\n5) Close with the goodnight: "teddy is sleepy — night-night, teddy." The whole routine chain, run by the smallest parent in the house.',
  say:'"Teddy bear! A soft brown bear. Hug him — gentle squeeze! Teddy is hungry… feed him… yummy, teddy! Now teddy\u2019s teeth — brush brush. And NOW teddy is sleepy… into the crib! Night-night, teddy. You take such good care of him."',
  notice:'On this card the words "teddy bear" sit under a brown plush bear with a round tummy — arms open for the hug that starts every lesson.',
  ask:'"Where is teddy?" "Can teddy have a hug?" "What does teddy need now — food? Brush? Bed?"',
  childSay:'Your child might feed teddy bite by bite, demand his toothbrush, or tuck him with a kiss — the smallest parent on the block, fully qualified.',
  real:'Bears ride in baskets, sit at cafés, visit clinics. Every outing with the bear is a rehearsal tour — "teddy is watching the doctor too!" Comfort travels; routines follow.',
  play:'Teddy\u2019s Full Day: a picture schedule for the bear — eat, play, wash, bed — and the child runs the whole day on schedule. A day parenting a teddy is a week of cooperation in the real routine.',
  check:'Ask: "Where is teddy?" "Give him a gentle hug!" "What does teddy do first at night?" (brush teeth / pajamas — any step named wins) — hug, care, rehearse: the friend is on the team.'},
 toothbrush:{
  title:'The Toothbrush Lesson',one:'Teach the brush ritual — child first, grown-up finishes, song as the timer.',
  seoTitle:'Toothbrush Lesson for Toddlers — Round and Round (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 2–3: how to teach "toothbrush" with the you-then-me handover, the four-line brush song and the twice-a-day habit for life.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "toothbrush" through the handover: the child brushes first (munching allowed), the grown-up finishes, and the same four-line song counts the whole thing down.',
  what:'The toothbrush is the blue brush with the yellow stripe — a twice-a-day-for-life tool, which makes the toddler years the perfect installation window.',
  mean:'Brushing fails as a fight and works as a ritual: the child goes first (control), the grown-up finishes (quality), the song is the timer (no clocks, no battles).',
  know:'You need the brush, a rice-grain of fluoride paste, the you-then-me order and the four-line song: "round and round, round and round, front and back, all done!" Spitting finishes the show.',
  why:'The handover order solves both problems at once — the child\u2019s need for control and the parent\u2019s need for clean teeth. And the song outlasts the resistance.',
  dev:'Oral-motor control, sequencing, the habit loop itself, and the tolerance for someone else\u2019s hands in their mouth that dental visits will need.',
  vocab:'toothbrush, teeth, brush, round and round, spit, all clean',
  teach:'1) Present at the sink: "toothbrush! Time for teeth."\n2) Rice-grain of paste on the brush — "tiny toothpaste, plenty!"\n3) Child brushes first: "your turn — round and round!" Munching the bristles is normal; keep going.\n4) The handover: "my turn — let me get the back ones." Quick, gentle, non-negotiable, done in twenty seconds.\n5) The song ends, spit, admire: "ALL CLEAN — sparkly smile!" Same order, both sessions, every day.',
  say:'"Toothbrush! Time to brush the teeth. Your turn first — round and round, round and round, front and back… my turn — the back ones, quick quick… spit! ALL CLEAN. Sparkly smile!"',
  notice:'On this card the word "toothbrush" sits under a blue and yellow brush — bristles up, ready for the twice-daily ceremony.',
  ask:'"Where is your toothbrush?" "Whose turn is first?" (mine!) "What do we do at the end?" (spit! all clean!)',
  childSay:'Your child might sing the brush song while eating, demand "MY turn first!", or brush the teddy\u2019s teeth with his own brush — the habit, spreading.',
  real:'Brushes live in cups, travel kits and hotel bags. A new setting is the ritual\u2019s test — same song, same order, any bathroom on Earth.',
  play:'Brush the Family: teddy\u2019s teeth, dolly\u2019s teeth, then the child\u2019s — the whole roster, in order, one song each. The clinic visit rehearses itself here.',
  check:'Ask: "What is this?" (toothbrush) "Who brushes first?" (me) "What does the song say?" (round and round!) — paste, brush, handover: installed for life.'},
 towel:{
  title:'The Towel Lesson',one:'Teach the big dry finish — head, tummy, toes, in the same order every bath.',
  seoTitle:'Towel Lesson for Toddlers — The Big Dry Hug (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 1–3: how to teach "towel" with the wrap-and-name drying order — head, tummy, toes — and the doll-drying rehearsal.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "towel" as the bath\u2019s grand finale: wrapped like a burrito, dried in the same named order every time — head, tummy, toes — and the body words come free.',
  what:'The towel is the big blue wrap that ends every bath — and the named drying order is one of the friendliest body-parts lessons ever disguised as a routine.',
  mean:'Drying in a fixed order does two jobs: the bath gets a predictable, cozy ending, and head-tummy-toes becomes a body map the child can recite on their own skin.',
  know:'You need the towel and the order: "towel dries the HEAD… towel dries the TUMMY… towel dries the TOES!" Same order every bath. The child\u2019s job: drying a doll the same way.',
  why:'The end of the bath is where escapes happen — the naked sprint down the hallway. A named, ceremonial drying routine catches the child inside the towel and the routine.',
  dev:'Body vocabulary, wrap tolerance (sensory), following a three-step order, and the care-task rehearsal that doll-drying provides.',
  vocab:'towel, dry, wrap, head, tummy, toes',
  teach:'1) Arms open at the bath\u2019s end: "towel time! Into the big dry hug."\n2) Wrap like a burrito, snuggle tight — the wrap IS the game.\n3) Dry in the named order, slow and warm: "towel dries the HEAD… the TUMMY… the TOES!"\n4) Hand over a second towel and a doll: "your turn — dry the dolly. Head… tummy… toes!"\n5) The exit: "all dry, all cozy — bath finished!" The same order next bath, and the body words stick for good.',
  say:'"Towel! Time to get dry — wrap you up like a burrito! Snug and warm. Towel dries the HEAD… towel dries the TUMMY… towel dries the TOES! All dry! Now you dry the dolly — head, tummy, toes!"',
  notice:'On this card the word "towel" sits under a folded blue towel — thick, soft and waiting at the end of every bath.',
  ask:'"Where does the towel dry first?" (the head!) "Where does it dry last?" (the toes) "Can you dry the dolly?"',
  childSay:'Your child might announce the order unprompted ("head! tummy! toes!"), wrap themselves, or dry every doll in the house — the ceremony, self-run.',
  real:'Towels follow swims, spills and rainy days. Beach towels, hand towels, the towel at grandma\u2019s — the word and the order travel to every bath and every pool.',
  play:'Towel Shop: every doll and bear gets a full wash-and-dry service — bath (a bowl), wrap, the named drying order, tipped well. The launderette never closes.',
  check:'Ask: "What dries you after the bath?" (the towel) "What dries first?" (head) "Show me the wrap!" — wrap, order, dry: cozy and complete.'},
 'toy-car':{
  title:'The Toy Car Lesson',one:'Teach the push-and-go — and the stop-and-go words that ride everywhere.',
  seoTitle:'Toy Car Lesson for Toddlers — Vroom and Stop (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 1–3: how to teach "toy car" with the cushion ramp, the shoebox garage and the stop-go game that drives first vocabulary.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "toy car" through the ramp-and-garage circuit — and hides the stop-and-go words inside the game, where they will drive every ride after this one.',
  what:'The toy car is the little red car with the blue wheels — usually the first vehicle a toddler pushes, and the first story with wheels on it.',
  mean:'Push-and-go teaches cause and effect; stop-and-go teaches the words that make play a language. The car is small enough to master and fast enough to love.',
  know:'You need the car, a cushion ramp and a shoebox garage. The script: "vroom vroom… STOP at the light… GO!" The words do the teaching; the car does the motivating.',
  why:'Traffic words (stop, go, fast, slow) are the most-reusable transport vocabulary in the set — they show up at every crossing, every bath cup, every spoon. Learned on a toy car, they are already home.',
  dev:'Pushing aim and force control, ramp physics (up the cushion, down the floor), stop-go inhibition (the freeze is a real skill), and first pretend narration.',
  vocab:'toy car, vroom, push, stop, go, fast, slow',
  teach:'1) The floor first: "toy car! Push it… vroom vroom!" Chase it together.\n2) Build the cushion ramp: "up the hill… and DOWN — wheee!" Repeat until physics feels owned.\n3) The shoebox garage: "park the car — night-night, car." Parking ends a lap; the lap can begin again.\n4) The words: "stop at the light… GO! Fast… slow… STOP!" The freeze is part of the game — hands off the car at STOP.\n5) Two cars, two players: one for you, one for them — the first shared race, with all the traffic words doing the talking.',
  say:'"Toy car! Vroom vroom — push it! Up the cushion… and down — wheee! Stop at the light… now GO! Fast, fast… slow, slow… STOP! Park it in the garage — night-night, car. Again!"',
  notice:'On this card the words "toy car" sit under a little red car with blue wheels — the exact shape of the first vehicle to cross a living-room floor.',
  ask:'"Where is the car?" "Make it GO!" "Freeze — what do we say?" (STOP!) "Fast or slow?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say "vroom!" while pushing, slam the car to a halt at STOP, or narrate the whole journey — the driver\u2019s licence, early.',
  real:'Cars are everywhere: the street, the car park, the pushchair\u2019s wheels. "Real cars go fast… SLOW at the crossing!" — the toy taught the street\u2019s words.',
  play:'Car Wash: the shoebox becomes a carwash — the car drives in, gets "sponged" with a facecloth, drives out sparkling. Wash, park, vroom: the full municipal service.',
  check:'Ask: "What is this?" (toy car) "Make it fast… now SLOW!" "Where does it sleep?" (the garage) — push, stop, park: driving is fluent.'},
 'toy-drum':{
  title:'The Toy Drum Lesson',one:'Teach loud and soft — the first music lesson, with the STOP rule built in.',
  seoTitle:'Toy Drum Lesson for Toddlers — Loud, Soft, Stop (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 1–3: how to teach "toy drum" with the copy-cat drumming game — loud, soft, fast, slow — and the stop rule said with a smile.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "toy drum" through the copy-cat game: loud, soft, fast, slow — the whole first music curriculum, ending on STOP, hands up, giggle.',
  what:'The toy drum is the red drum with the yellow rims and two sticks — the loudest friend in the room and the first instrument a toddler can truly play.',
  mean:'Drumming is listening practice disguised as noise: copying a rhythm demands attention. The loud-soft contrast also plants the inside-voice idea in a place where it is FUN.',
  know:'You need the drum, the sticks and the copy-cat rule: I play, you play. The four contrasts — loud/soft, fast/slow — then STOP, sticks up. The stop is part of the music.',
  why:'A child who can play loud AND soft owns both volumes. That ownership is the foundation the inside voice gets built on — and the drum is where it is the most fun to practice.',
  dev:'Rhythm copying (attention!), wrist control, dynamic contrast (loud/soft), inhibition on the stop, and the listening half of all music.',
  vocab:'toy drum, bang, loud, soft, fast, slow, stop',
  teach:'1) Introduce the sticks: "toy drum! Bang bang BANG — loud!"\n2) The contrast: "now softly… soft, soft taps." Model with a whisper-face.\n3) Copy-cat: you play loud, they play loud; soft, soft; fast; slow. Four rounds, four contrasts.\n4) The stop: "and… STOP! Sticks up!" Freeze with the sticks in the air — the funniest rule in music.\n5) The encore: their turn to lead, you copy. The teacher seat is the real prize.',
  say:'"Toy drum! Bang bang BANG — loud! Now soft… soft, soft. Copy me — loud! …soft! Fast fast fast… slooow. And… STOP! Sticks up high! Your turn — I\u2019ll copy YOU!"',
  notice:'On this card the words "toy drum" sit under a red drum with yellow rims and two crossed sticks — the volume of the room, about to change.',
  ask:'"Bang LOUD!" "Now soft…" "What do we say at the end?" (STOP!) "Your turn — teach me!"',
  childSay:'Your child might lead the copy-cat game, whisper-tap theatrically, or freeze mid-bang with sticks aloft — dynamics, mastered.',
  real:'Loud and soft live everywhere: the vacuum (loud), the cat (soft), the library (whispers). "Drum rules — soft inside!" transfers the whole lesson to daily life.',
  play:'The Volume Song: bang the four contrasts in a fixed order with the song, ending on STOP — the same order daily until the child plays it solo for an audience of teddies.',
  check:'Ask: "What is this?" (toy drum) "Show me loud… now soft!" "Where do the sticks go at STOP?" (up high!) — bang, contrast, stop: the first concert, complete.'},
 'toy-train':{
  title:'The Toy Train Lesson',one:'Teach the journey game — over, under, through, stop at the station.',
  seoTitle:'Toy Train Lesson for Toddlers — Choo Choo, All Aboard (Toddler Room)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Toddler Room lesson for ages 1–3: how to teach "toy train" with the journey game — cushion mountains, chair tunnels and the station stop that ends every lap.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches "toy train" as a journey: over the cushion mountain, through the chair tunnel, stop at the station — position words riding behind the little green engine.',
  what:'The toy train is the green engine with its red trim and two carriages — wheels that connect, a line that grows, and the chugga-chugga that carries position words for free.',
  mean:'The train game is a preposition festival: over, under, through, round, stop. Every lap lands the words again — and the station stop teaches journeys have endings.',
  know:'You need the train and a course: cushion mountain (over), chair tunnel (under), rug lake (round). The station is a shoebox. The words name each obstacle as the train meets it.',
  why:'Position words are the grammar of a toddler\u2019s physical world — and the train repeats them with the enthusiasm only a chuffa-chuffa can supply.',
  dev:'Position/spatial words, tracking a moving object, sequencing (mountain, then tunnel, then station), and the pretend-narration that stories grow from.',
  vocab:'toy train, choo choo, over, under, stop, station',
  teach:'1) The first lap together: "toy train! Choo choo — chuffa chuffa chuffa!" Push it round the rug.\n2) The mountain: "OVER the cushion — up and over!" Name it as the wheels climb.\n3) The tunnel: "UNDER the chair — through the dark! …Out the other side!"\n4) The station: "STOP at the station — all aboard! Next stop: the sofa!" Passengers (small toys) board; the service expands.\n5) The child drives the route solo, narrating if they can — you ride as a passenger and ask: "what comes next?"',
  say:'"Toy train! Choo choo! Chuffa chuffa… OVER the cushion mountain… UNDER the chair tunnel… round the rug lake… and STOP at the station! All aboard! Next stop — the sofa! Chuffa chuffa choo choo!"',
  notice:'On this card the words "toy train" sit under a green engine with red trim and two little carriages — coupled and ready, the journey about to start.',
  ask:'"Where does the train go?" (over! under! round!) "What does the train say?" (choo choo!) "Where does the journey end?" (the station)',
  childSay:'Your child might say "choo choo!" through the tunnel, invent new stops ("grandma\u2019s!"), or couple more carriages on — the network, expanding.',
  real:'Trains run through every town: the level crossing, the platform, the track under the bridge. Watching a real train after the toy is a revelation — "choo choo, but ENORMOUS!"',
  play:'Night Train: dim the lights, the engine\u2019s headlamp (a torch) leads the route — over, under, through, station. Same words, new drama. Passengers: everyone, teddies included.',
  check:'Ask: "What is this?" (toy train) "Where does it go?" (over, under, round) "Where does it stop?" (the station) — chuff, climb, stop: the journey is fluent.'}
};
